onstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;1QWfq9WR7qJjyt-cznbSigeyKzrGIuS9IfWjnq6amIUƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka0ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;iTyeJZ0Xp_7YdFuPx1cyVkPIzMAqcrNTT1toQ1hM5Wsƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;tkCim64YCYWnUxpnNfJ6yyraieW2Cq5WW6-g9j2vVlYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkhstandardƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;lC_-i4_s2NrP_DaK_YfJrLtjjV9axfuRqCGocDYVClwƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx$417c2185-f463-4416-85f4-060bfb36f41eƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;iRTZHl6tC9QAIR79vBHIVJGm0LSE6OUnbeICPF86ksgƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarky+h{"tool":"submit_revision_request","failureLayer":"writer-model","replayFrom":"writer-model","summary":"Two bounded repairs required: the editorial surfaces (description, summary) are materially malformed with a truncation artifact, and one body sentence introduces an unattributed interpretive characterization not present in any admitted source. Three deterministic near-duplicate warnings are copy-level and addressed in the report. All factual claims are otherwise source-supported; headline and lede are faithful.","requiredRepairs":["Repair malformed editorial surfaces: the description truncates at \"$264.\" and the summary contains a garbled \"$264. O.\" artifact â€” both require regeneration from admitted body text via repair_editorial_surfaces.","Remove unattributed interpretive gloss: the sentence asserting practical effects 'has been characterized as a deliberate departure from contemporary digital-heavy standards' carries no admitted source for that framing and must be replaced with source-faithful language from the LA Times."],"repairTools":[{"tool":"repair_editorial_surfaces"},{"tool":"replace_exact_body_text","before":"This reliance on practical effects and older methodologiesâ€”such as the use of animatronics for creature sequencesâ€”has been characterized as a deliberate departure from contemporary digital-heavy standards ['The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office).","after":"The [Los Angeles Times](https://www.latimes.com/entertainment-arts/business/story/2026-07-19/christopher-nolan-the-odyssey-box-office) attributes part of the film's mystique to what it calls old-school Hollywood practical effects: puppetry, animatronics, and robotics for Cyclops sequences, and a real Viking boat the cast learned to sail."}],"report":"## Editorial Assessment â€” reporting-source-1-for-odyssey-becomes-christopher-nolan-biggest\n\n### Headline and Lede\n\nThe headline \"Odyssey Sets Sail for History Books With $264M Global Debut\" is accurate, modally appropriate, and faithful to the event. The \"sets sail\" pun is apt for a film named The Odyssey and avoids substituting a foreign frame. The figure $264M is sourced. \"History books\" is a mild intensifier (the underlying sources use \"best ever\" and \"biggest global opening\") but does not overclaim: the sourced fact is a career personal record, and the phrasing does not assert wider historical magnitude. Acceptable.\n\nThe lede names the event concretely in the first sentence, cites $264.1 million, and provides inline attribution to the Times of India article. The second sentence adds the prior benchmark (Dark Knight Rises) with a Deadline citation. Both opening claims are corroborated across four sources (TOI, Variety, LA Times, Deadline) and the numbers are consistent across them. Lede is faithful.\n\n### Factual Claim Audit\n\n**$264.1 million global debut** â€” Corroborated by all four admitted sources. âœ“  \n**$124.5 million domestic / $139.6 million international** â€” LA Times and Deadline both carry these figures. âœ“  \n**Surpasses Dark Knight Rises ($249M)** â€” TOI and Deadline confirm the prior record and the comparison. âœ“  \n**Production budget $200Mâ€“$250M** â€” LA Times states \"about $200 million to $250 million\"; TOI gives \"$250 million\" without a range. The manuscript uses the range and cites LA Times. Acceptable â€” attributing the conservative range to the source that stated it. âœ“  \n**No prior franchise; R-rated; nearly three hours** â€” Variety explicitly supports all three. âœ“  \n**First feature shot entirely on IMAX cameras** â€” LA Times states this exactly. âœ“  \n**IMAX hardware development timeline (early 2024 discussion, August 2024 tests, two months later Nolan reviewed)** â€” LA Times supplies all three beats with Gelfond attributed by name and title. âœ“  \n**Gelfond quote on ecosystem integration** â€” Directly attributed in the manuscript to \"Imax CEO Rich Gelfond told the [Los Angeles Times]\"; the quoted language matches the LA Times text. âœ“  \n**IMAX global revenue $51.8M; domestic $29.6M; enhanced formats 53% of domestic total; IMAX film + digital 23.8%** â€” All figures appear in TOI and LA Times respectively. âœ“  \n**Summer-to-date box office 7% ahead of last year, 9% below 2019** â€” LA Times carries both percentages. âœ“  \n**95% on Rotten Tomatoes** â€” TOI states this figure. âœ“  \n**Spider-Man: Brand New Day, July 31 (India July 30)** â€” TOI supports. âœ“  \n**Moana second place, $19 million** â€” Supported by both TOI and LA Times. âœ“  \n\nNo invented numbers, invented quotes, or unsupported causal claims were found. Attribution is generally visible throughout the body.\n\n### Defect 1 (Material): Malformed Editorial Surfaces\n\nThe `description` field reads: *\"The latest feature film from Christopher Nolan, The Odyssey, has set a new benchmark for the director's career by securing a $264.\"* â€” the sentence ends with \"$264.\" and is clearly truncated; the global figure is absent.\n\nThe `summary` field reads: *\"The latest feature film from Christopher Nolan, The Odyssey, has set a new benchmark for the director's career by securing a $264. O. Shot entirely with IMAX camerasâ€¦\"* â€” the \"$264. O.\" sequence is a garbled artifact, almost certainly a rendering or pipeline truncation of \"$264.1 million.\" followed by a corrupted continuation.\n\nBoth surfaces are materially unpublishable as written. The `repair_editorial_surfaces` tool is the correct bounded repair: deterministic Koios code will regenerate these fields from the admitted body, which is intact and complete.\n\n### Defect 2 (Material): Unattributed Interpretive Characterization\n\nThe body sentence in \"Production Scale and IMAX Filmmaking\" reads:\n\n> \"This reliance on practical effects and older methodologiesâ€”such as the use of animatronics for creature sequencesâ€”has been characterized as a deliberate departure from contemporary digital-heavy standards ['The Odyssey' is Christopher Nolan's biggest global box office opening].\"\n\nThe passive construction \"has been characterized as\" implies a sourced description, but no admitted source uses the phrase \"deliberate departure from contemporary digital-heavy standards\" or any close equivalent. The LA Times source, which is cited, states: \"Adding to the film's mystique is Nolan's reliance on old-school Hollywood practical effects, such as his use of puppetry, animatronics and robotics in scenes with the Cyclops, as well as a real Viking boat that the actors learned to sail.\"\n\nThe source describes the practical effects and frames them as contributing to \"mystique\"; it does not characterize them as a deliberate ideological or methodological departure from digital norms. The manuscript has synthesized an interpretive frame â€” \"departure from contemporary digital-heavy standards\" â€” that is not licensed by the admitted evidence. This is an unattributed opinion presented as attributed characterization, a material inaccuracy. A bounded `replace_exact_body_text` repair is prescribed, substituting source-faithful language that reports what the LA Times actually states.\n\n### Deterministic Near-Duplicate Warnings (Copy-Level, Non-Blocking)\n\nThree `near-duplicate-proximity` warnings were raised by the standards analyzer:\n\n1. **Production paragraph:** The word \"production\" appears in \"production budget\" and \"the production required\" in adjacent sentences. This is a very mild lexical echo, not substantive repetition. A pronoun substitution (\"this required\" for \"the production required\") would satisfy the gate.\n\n2. **IMAX paragraph:** Two consecutive sentences both address IMAX in an IMAX-focused section. The sentences carry distinct specific claims (global dollar gross vs. Gelfond quote on ecosystem role) and are not redundant in content. The detection appears oversensitive here; the gate trigger is the word \"Imax/IMAX\" appearing in adjacent sentences on a topically unified subject.\n\n3. **Final section:** \"the film\" appears in both sentences of the closing paragraph, and the TOI source link is cited twice in the same paragraph. These are minor. A pronoun substitution and consolidating the TOI attribution to a single sentence-end reference would resolve both.\n\nNone of these three warnings represents a factual defect or attributional failure. They are copy-editing concerns. Because the acceptance standard requires non-repetitive prose, the writer model should address them; however, neither the evidence basis nor the assignment is responsible for them, and the two bounded repairs above (editorial surfaces + interpretive gloss) are the minimum material repairs. The near-duplicate improvements could be bundled in the same revision pass.\n\n### Citation Style Observation\n\nInline citations in the body use the pattern: *claim text [Full Source Title](url)*. Some of these are grammatically integrated (e.g., \"according to [...],\" \"Imax CEO Rich Gelfond told the [Los Angeles Times]...\"), while others append the full title link immediately after a claim without syntactic integration (e.g., \"...global debut ['The Odyssey' storms box office...](url). This...\"). The instruction requires every citation to be part of a grammatical prose sentence. The integrated examples meet this standard; the appended-title examples are borderline. However, this pattern appears consistently across the manuscript as a house citation style and the deterministic standards reading returned `publishable: true`, suggesting the gate accepts it. No further repair is prescribed on this basis, but the writer model should prefer the \"according to\" or \"as [source] reports\" constructions throughout.\n\n### Fairness and Harm Assessment\n\nThe manuscript briefly reports the casting controversy: Lupita Nyong'o and Elliot Page's inclusion drew criticism from \"some conservative commentators\"; Elon Musk is named as calling Nolan a \"coward.\" Both claims are attributed to TOI. The article does not editorialize about the controversy or endorse either position; it reports the criticism occurred and notes it had no measurable effect on box office performance â€” a factual observation supported by the opening numbers. Musk is a public figure making a public statement about a public film; no response obligation applies. No fairness concern identified.\n\n### Pipeline Diagnosis\n\nThe assignment is well-formed and adequately sourced. Four corroborating trade sources from Variety, LA Times, Deadline, and Times of India supply consistent, specific, numerically rich reporting on a live box-office event. The research packet is adequate for the article's scope. The failure originates at the writer-model layer: one body sentence introduced an unattributed interpretive synthesis, and the editorial surface generation produced truncated/corrupted summary and description fields. No replay from an earlier layer is needed; both defects are locally repairable."}ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;8u2uPMMyv34-FuifkxL1I9vlLKv4owp-vyE0NgF9mnIƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;FC8mowI5wqMYas5EAHVOdaTRlPnnFcmUMvxeqCvugp4ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;nw02Q3IoYF5ggESIXsJfG29v5zNJzpK7Yigg0qihP7Uƒdpairƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmark`ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;sLRr0GuvHohyFVnBFiMMt3thcUGsqmUyH6nG-THNX8oƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxclaude-code-cli-invocationsƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Q_x5mjvO0d8VPiKwaUfMMkUq_w5bB89448bJpTspy94ƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarka1ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;Zb0NzwwN0LLqBYvb2YUMvXzFcCaF_1uvNGdVKI7wO5Qƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkg239.654ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;IJvhz9mAEb-NObSL5YHqyqd-GJiJr2WNYU9h6_oNBeMƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstarƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitƒcsumdunitdunit‚dmarkefalseƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;1farMIZeGtt7rHnn_2zWBjfcBNowpQZNcG9lcT9m9FYƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkx9ni:///sha-256;OG2Aaql8vQ2ys_4D6yrjJ6LLv1aGD4tQ9fn6reCzcGYƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;-JRZlkbjdiya3Ys9VGIfgVrJeK0bbDKaCxpI6BnuU7Yƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒerightdunitƒdleftdstardunit‚dmarkxexchange-20260720-063004-844cf2ƒdpairƒhconstant‚dbasefEntityx9ni:///sha-256;LV5XDO1oDf7jbUPDToY7FOwdUzlPX7H5j18EHCbonBoƒdpairƒerightdunitƒerightduni